// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
// Glasnost's own rules patch layer "Pathing and other fixes" (M8 step C7). It holds no game
// data: it only sets quirk switches of our rules format to their off position, the behaviour
// each switch's spec states for that position (PLAN.md D6 and 4.6). The defaults in
// crates/deterministic/rules/src/quirks.rs stay the original's behaviour (D6, D8); this layer
// is an option of the client's new-game setup and network lobby, applied through
// `glasnost_rules::resolve_ruleset` as a mod patch layer, so it is part of `sim_rules_hash`.
//
// An entry is admitted only when (1) an approved spec states the switch's off position and the
// engine implements it, (2) the on position reads as a bug in play: a play note or a request
// names it, or the approved spec's own Edge cases or quirk text states its effect as a unit
// stuck, giving up although an open way exists, never moving again, or a visible glitch, and
// (3) it is not a balance change (D14). Each entry cites its claim and what it answers. The list
// is kept short; docs/m8-claims/c7-play-fixes.md gives the entries considered and rejected.
//
// Our own pathing improvements (the lead's decision of 2026-10-29, M8 step C7 parts 4 and 5)
// are admitted without condition (1): their off position is our design, marked
// `// design: C7-P4 <name>` or `// design: C7-P5 <name>` in the code and described in
// docs/m8-claims/c7-pathing.md, which also gives the synthetic scenario that shows the bug in
// the on position. Conditions (2) and (3) still hold.
{
  "globals": {
    "quirks": {
      // S-0212 claim 13 and Edge cases (quirks.corridor_retry_no_fallback off): when the new
      // coarse search after a corridor retry fails, one more cell search without a corridor is
      // made, with the same limits as the others, and the path computation fails only if it
      // fails too. On (the original), the computation fails and the unit gives up at once
      // although an open way round exists (claim 23 row 2).
      // Answers: the spec's own Edge case (a short strip of ground the unit cannot enter, open
      // to the coarse searches, fails at some alignments; off, it is passed at every one).
      // Pathing reports 1 and 2 (docs/m8-claims/a4-play-notes.md), Q-1771 and Q-1870, for
      // which this entry was admitted, are answered by S-0212 r82: the original finds the way
      // out of its own base on the first cell search, and this entry changes nothing there.
      "corridor_retry_no_fallback": false,
      // S-0212 Edge cases (quirks.corridor_free_part_ignores_height off): the free part and its
      // borders apply the height rule of claim 10, so a ledge (a line of flat cells one level
      // above flat ground) stops the free part as a strip does and the retries lead a new coarse
      // search round it. On (the original), the same cell search fails 5 times at a ledge and
      // the unit refuses the order although an open way round exists.
      // Answers: the spec's own Edge case (a ledge across the route), the same gameplay impact
      // as the strip above.
      "corridor_free_part_ignores_height": false,
      // S-0314 Edge cases (Hovering vehicle stranded) and Terms, What ends it
      // (quirks.depot_loss_strands_hover off): losing contact with a depot ends being settled,
      // so a hovering vehicle is switched on again and rises. On (the original), a hover whose
      // depot is destroyed or sold after it became settled never moves again once down
      // (claim 22), and so does one ordered away while it still sinks at a depot that comes
      // down outside the depot's cells: its order released the contact, and no later order
      // ends being settled there. Off, that order's release (claim 20), or the end of service
      // for a docked hover, ends it.
      // Answers: the play seam of docs/m8-claims/intake30-S-0314.md (a hover that never moves
      // again) and the spec's own Edge case.
      "depot_loss_strands_hover": false,
      // S-0402 claim 11 and Edge cases (Unload without contact stalls; quirks.unload_without_contact_stalls
      // off): a harvester whose refinery breaks off contact while it unloads without a sale
      // falls back to rest as if unload were not current and starts what S-0215 claim 15 gives
      // it. On (the original), it stays in unload for good, processed every tick, until an
      // order gives it a behaviour. Acts only in that stalled state.
      "unload_without_contact_stalls": false,
      // S-0605 claim 17 and Edge cases (Contact outlives enter; quirks.absorber_stale_contact_kept
      // off): an actor in contact with an absorbing building that has neither enter current nor
      // enter queued gives the contact up in its own turn. On (the original), it keeps the
      // building's link place for good and every later entrant waits in line for ever.
      "absorber_stale_contact_kept": false,
      // S-0503 claim 19 and Edge cases (quirks.hover_landing_request_stands off): a landing-first
      // in-place hover treats its standing landing request as met once it is no longer moving, so
      // its next deploy step deploys it. On (the original), it never deploys; unload stays
      // current with a deploy step each tick.
      "hover_landing_request_stands": false,
      // Our design, C7-P4 stalled blockers (quirks.path_through_stalled_blockers off; S-0212
      // claims 8 and 22): a unit stalled for the blockage delay in a deadlock (stalled units
      // that wait on each other's cells), or waiting on one, first looks for a way that keeps
      // off the cells of the deadlock's other units, and takes the original's path only when
      // there is none; a queue whose head still moves is left alone. On (the original), two
      // groups that meet head-on in a passage two cells wide re-path through each other for
      // ever and none arrives.
      // Answers: the scenario "corridor 2, 6 each way" of docs/m8-claims/c7-pathing.md.
      "path_through_stalled_blockers": false,
      // Our design, C7-P5 yield (quirks.deadlock_no_yield off; S-0212 claims 8 and 22): in a
      // deadlock of stalled units with no way round (no detour found, and a fill round the
      // deadlock from the unit or from its destination is shut in), the units near it heading
      // against the one with the lowest object id give way: each goes to the nearest free cell
      // off the other side's way, waits until the other side has arrived or passed it (at most
      // 900 ticks) and resumes its order. A deadlock with a way round keeps C7-P4's detour. It
      // needs path_through_stalled_blockers off, which finds the deadlock. On (the original),
      // two groups that meet in a passage with no way round stay stuck. Answers: the scenario
      // "two rooms, 6 each way" of docs/m8-claims/c7-pathing.md.
      "deadlock_no_yield": false,
      // Our design, C7-P4 nearest reachable cell (quirks.unreachable_move_dropped off; S-0212
      // claim 23): a move whose computation fails tries once more towards the nearest cell
      // the unit can stand on in its own reach region. On (the original), units sent to a cell
      // they cannot reach (inside rock, or walled in by buildings) do not move at all.
      // Answers: the scenarios "unreachable cell" and "inside buildings" of the same ledger.
      "unreachable_move_dropped": false,
      // Our design, C7-P4 group spread (quirks.group_spread_no_detour off; S-0213 claim 7 and
      // Edge cases): a group member whose look gives no cell of its own gets the nearest open
      // cell round the common cell instead of a cell already given. On (the original), groups
      // moved next to water or buildings send several members to one cell, where they jostle
      // and give up (the spec's own Edge case).
      // Answers: the scenarios "by buildings" and "shore" of the same ledger.
      "group_spread_no_detour": false,
      // Our design, C7-P5 rally spread (quirks.rally_point_shared off; S-0417 claims 8 and 10,
      // S-0404 claim 13): each unit a factory releases with a cell rally point is sent to a cell
      // of its own round it (the rally cell while no other unit of the house has it, else the
      // group spread's nearest open cell round it). On (the original), every released unit
      // heads for the one rally cell, where they crowd and give up after arriving.
      // Answers: the scenario "factory stream" of docs/m8-claims/c7-pathing.md.
      "rally_point_shared": false,
      // Our design, C7-P5 field cells (quirks.harvest_cell_shared off; S-0402 claim 4): a
      // harvester's ring search does not accept an ore cell another unit of its house is already
      // heading for. On (the original), harvesters sent to one field from one place pick the same
      // cells, block each other one step short of them and give up the move.
      // Answers: the scenario "harvesters" of docs/m8-claims/c7-pathing.md.
      "harvest_cell_shared": false,
      // S-0108 claims 11 and 18 and Edge cases (quirks.ungranted_super_reads_clock off): a
      // superweapon building's slots count a record that is not granted as not charged, so its
      // grand opening starts super-1 and the charge check waits for the grant. On (the
      // original), a building that opens with a record never granted, as at most set-up
      // placements, "shows the charged super-3 although nothing has charged" (the spec's own
      // quirk text: a visible glitch). In our engine these slots are the client's, so the
      // switch changes no simulation state (docs/m8-claims/c7-play-fixes.md, intake 53).
      "ungranted_super_reads_clock": false,
      // S-0306 claim 3 (b) and Edge cases (quirks.switched_off_hover_lead off): a hovering
      // vehicle that is switched off is not led, so shots at it aim at its reference point. On
      // (the original), one that S-0503 still counts as moving (a heading point kept) is led
      // along its body facing although it has stopped travelling, and the spec's own quirk text
      // states that attackers that keep it as their target "can then fail to destroy it for the
      // rest of the game" (a stuck attacker). Acts only on switched-off hovering targets.
      "switched_off_hover_lead": false,
      // S-0501 claims 4, 10 and 51 and Edge cases (quirks.aircraft_edge_hold off): an aircraft
      // that may leave the map now is not pushed back at the legal map's boundary, whatever its
      // flags, so it takes no candidate and no draw there, and one that claim 10 would not remove
      // before it leaves the legal map is removed in the update tick in which its position leaves
      // it. On (the original), one with the fly-back flag, or with neither flag at an edge where
      // the usable area's boundary lies on or beyond the legal map's, is never removed there: a
      // spy plane in spy-plane overfly "stays at the edge it met for the rest of the game"
      // (claim 51, Q-2291), with a scenario draw in each tick its step would leave the map.
      // Acts only on aircraft that may leave the map now.
      "aircraft_edge_hold": false,
      // S-0209 claims 4 and 9 and Edge cases (quirks.walker_attacks_unhittable_obstacle off): rows
      // WP13, WP24 and WP28 give blocked instead of enemy obstacle when the firing check without
      // the range test, for the weapon the walker would use against the occupant, answers
      // illegal, so the path search does not consider that step and the walker routes round the
      // occupant. On (the original), an armed walker whose path leads through an enemy building,
      // closed gate or other enemy object that its weapon cannot fire at takes it as its target,
      // loses it at the next check and rests in front of it without its move, or under an attack
      // move takes and loses it again for as long as it is engaged (Q-2396, filed from the AI
      // quick lane: an infantry unit held in front of an enemy building for about 1,260 ticks).
      // Acts only on occupants the walker's weapon cannot fire at.
      "walker_attacks_unhittable_obstacle": false,
    },
  },
}
